1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,429
Coach: Disagreement can help a group examine a proposal.

2
00:00:03,429 --> 00:00:06,857
Coach: First, make clear what the difference is about.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:13,714
Coach: Rejecting a whole topic when you disagree with one detail makes that difference harder to discuss.

4
00:00:14,064 --> 00:00:19,635
Coach: Imagine a group planning a twenty-minute practice session with two sample problems.

5
00:00:19,635 --> 00:00:25,207
Coach: The aim is to understand the key reasoning, not simply finish more answers.

6
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:26,921
Coach: In this fictional case,

7
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:32,064
Coach: an available tool can show a hint or a full worked answer.

8
00:00:32,064 --> 00:00:33,778
Coach: Its output is unverified.

9
00:00:34,128 --> 00:00:38,414
Maya: An answer tool can save time when we get stuck.

10
00:00:38,414 --> 00:00:43,128
Maya: I would use it as soon as a problem becomes difficult.

11
00:00:43,478 --> 00:00:47,764
Coach: Maya offers a possible benefit and a proposal about timing.

12
00:00:47,764 --> 00:00:52,478
Coach: Saving time is her reason; immediate use is the proposed action.

13
00:00:52,478 --> 00:00:58,478
Coach: No practice session has happened, and the group has not agreed to this plan.

14
00:00:58,828 --> 00:01:02,685
Coach: You might value saving time while questioning that timing.

15
00:01:02,685 --> 00:01:04,828
Coach: Those positions can fit together.

16
00:01:04,828 --> 00:01:12,114
Coach: Disagreeing about when to use a tool does not require denying every possible benefit it might offer.

17
00:01:12,464 --> 00:01:15,892
Coach: There is also something Maya has not specified:

18
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:20,178
Coach: whether she means a hint or the full worked answer.

19
00:01:20,178 --> 00:01:23,607
Coach: The tool can offer either in this scenario.

20
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:27,035
Coach: Do not silently choose a mode for her.

21
00:01:27,385 --> 00:01:31,242
Coach: Privately separate what is stated from what remains open.

22
00:01:31,242 --> 00:01:37,242
Coach: What timing has Maya proposed, and which kind of help has she left unspecified?

23
00:01:37,242 --> 00:01:40,242
Coach: Keep both questions visible while you think.

24
00:01:48,592 --> 00:01:50,735
Coach: The immediate timing is explicit.

25
00:01:50,735 --> 00:01:53,306
Coach: The kind of help is not.

26
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:55,878
Coach: You can question the timing directly,

27
00:01:55,878 --> 00:02:00,592
Coach: or clarify the mode if that distinction matters to your concern.

28
00:02:00,592 --> 00:02:03,592
Coach: You do not have to do both.

29
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:06,085
Coach: Direct disagreement can be respectful.

30
00:02:06,085 --> 00:02:09,514
Coach: The important distinction is between challenging a proposal

31
00:02:09,514 --> 00:02:12,514
Coach: and judging the person who offered it.

32
00:02:12,514 --> 00:02:15,942
Coach: A calm voice does not replace a reason,

33
00:02:15,942 --> 00:02:20,228
Coach: and a clear objection need not be disguised as agreement.

34
00:02:20,578 --> 00:02:25,721
You: I would not make difficulty alone the trigger for using the tool.

35
00:02:25,721 --> 00:02:30,007
You: I would like us to try a step ourselves first.

36
00:02:30,357 --> 00:02:32,928
Coach: This reply identifies a timing difference.

37
00:02:32,928 --> 00:02:38,071
Coach: It does not call Maya lazy or claim that tools prevent learning.

38
00:02:38,071 --> 00:02:41,500
Coach: The proposed first attempt still needs a reason

39
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,071
Coach: and consideration against the available time.

40
00:02:44,421 --> 00:02:49,992
Coach: From Maya’s words, you cannot know that she only cares about finishing quickly.

41
00:02:49,992 --> 00:02:52,135
Coach: Her stated reason concerns time.

42
00:02:52,135 --> 00:02:56,849
Coach: The group can examine whether her suggestion serves the shared aim

43
00:02:56,849 --> 00:02:58,992
Coach: without inventing a hidden motive.

44
00:02:59,342 --> 00:03:04,913
Coach: Likewise, using help immediately would not by itself prove that someone learned nothing.

45
00:03:04,913 --> 00:03:10,056
Coach: It might support understanding, or leave less room for an independent attempt.

46
00:03:10,056 --> 00:03:14,342
Coach: We need a more precise concern than a blanket judgement.

47
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:19,406
Coach: Keep the task in view: understanding the reasoning within twenty minutes.

48
00:03:19,406 --> 00:03:24,978
Coach: The issue is how a proposed use of help might serve that aim,

49
00:03:24,978 --> 00:03:27,549
Coach: with limited time and unverified output.

50
00:03:27,899 --> 00:03:31,756
Coach: Choose one part of Maya’s proposal you would examine:

51
00:03:31,756 --> 00:03:36,899
Coach: the timing, the kind of help, or the reason it should help.

52
00:03:36,899 --> 00:03:39,899
Coach: Form a short question or objection privately.

53
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:42,042
Coach: Nothing is recorded or assessed.

54
00:03:50,392 --> 00:03:55,535
Coach: If you tried a reply, check whether it names the actual difference.

55
00:03:55,535 --> 00:03:58,964
Coach: If you did not, you can return later.

56
00:03:58,964 --> 00:04:01,964
Coach: The aim is not to sound opposed;

57
00:04:01,964 --> 00:04:05,821
Coach: it is to make a concern available for discussion.

58
00:04:06,171 --> 00:04:11,314
Coach: Next, we will look at recognising a reason without pretending to agree.

59
00:04:11,314 --> 00:04:14,314
Coach: You can take a person’s point seriously

60
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:17,314
Coach: while still questioning the action they suggest.
